//
// loft#1207's CONTROL half, REFUSAL side. A keyed LOCAL and its nullable twin refuse a vector
// source the collection cannot hold, and the `?` on the declaration does not buy the second one
// past it.
//
// The fix for loft#1207 peels the optional inside `is_collection`, so a `vector<τ>?` stops being
// the one collection that predicate denied. The risk a peel carries is reaching FURTHER than the
// defect, and this file is where that would show: the two spellings must move together, so a
// build where the nullable one compiles and the dense one does not has widened the peel past
// the rule.
//
// Refused by `types.md` `C-Only` — `⤳` is the only implicit coercion, so a `vector<Fk1207b>`
// has no reading as records of a `hash<Ek1207b[k]>`. That is a real type error under any
// spelling, which is what makes it a stable control.
//
// ⚠ THIS FILE PINNED THE WRONG SHAPE UNTIL loft#1237, and the way it went wrong is the reason
// the cells changed. It asserted that both locals refuse a vector source of the ELEMENT type,
// and called that refusal "the documented spelling" — but `formal/collections.md` states what
// `(Col-Insert)`'s source may be, and a `vector` OF elements is the third licensed shape, placed
// one by one by a keyed kind (loft#1159). The rule names exactly two shapes it does NOT license
// — a whole keyed collection into another, and a bare element into a vector — and that was
// neither. So the refusal was a deviation, this file was holding it in place, and the guard read
// green while a keyed FIELD accepted the identical statement.
//
// A control is only as good as the reason under it. This one carried "dense and nullable AGREE",
// which was right and is kept, welded to "and both refuse", which was never a rule. Only the
// second clause moved: the source became one the collection genuinely cannot hold.
//
// The ACCEPT half of the same agreement — both spellings taking a vector of the element type —
// runs in `1237-a-keyed-local-takes-a-vector-of-its-elements.loft`, and it has to live there
// rather than here: a firing `@EXPECT_ERROR` stops a file before it executes, so an assertion in
// any other function of this file would be compiled and never run (`wrap.rs` R1). That is the
// `<n>`/`<n>b` split the corpus already uses, and it is why loft#1207's own positive half is in
// `1207-a-nullable-collection-appends-a-non-literal-source.loft`.
//
// @falsified-at: eb14372e — MEASURED, and the channel is `check_diagnostics`, not the exit.
//   Both cells report the same diagnostic on the parent as on this build, which is the POINT of
//   a control: it is the cell that must not move, so a guard that only passed after a fix would
//   be measuring the wrong thing. What makes the file falsifiable is the opposite build — with
//   the peel widened to admit an unholdable source, both `@EXPECT_ERROR` lines go unmatched and
//   `tests/wrap.rs` fails the file.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.
//   CHANNEL: `check_diagnostics` — the diagnostic each cell reports, never the exit code.
//   This file is a CONTROL, so both cells must report the SAME diagnostic on the parent as
//   here; what falsifies it is the opposite build, where a widened peel leaves its
//   `@EXPECT_ERROR` lines unmatched. A run scored on values or exit learns nothing about it.

struct Ek1207b { k: integer, nm: text }
struct Fk1207b { j: integer }

// A DENSE keyed local: a vector of the WRONG element type has no reading here.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot change type from hash
fn test_1207b_dense_keyed_local_refuses_an_unholdable_vector() {
  bad1207b: vector<Fk1207b> = [Fk1207b { j: 1 }];
  d1207b: hash<Ek1207b[k]> = [];
  d1207b += bad1207b;
  assert(len(d1207b) == 1, "unreached");
}

// The NULLABLE twin one `?` over: the peel must not make this one compile.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: cannot change type from hash
fn test_1207b_nullable_keyed_local_refuses_an_unholdable_vector() {
  bad1207c: vector<Fk1207b> = [Fk1207b { j: 2 }];
  d1207c: hash<Ek1207b[k]>? = [];
  d1207c += bad1207c;
  assert(len(d1207c?) == 1, "unreached");
}
